{
  "id": "carriacou_big_drum",
  "name": "Big Drum (Carriacou Nation Dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ancestral drum-dance of Carriacou in the Grenadines, whose dances are grouped by the African nations from which islanders trace descent (Cromanti, Igbo, Manding, Chamba, Moko, Temne, Arada, Kongo and others) alongside Creole dances such as juba and kalenda. It is held at tombstone feasts, boat launchings, weddings and community maroon feasts and opens with the Beg Pardon; three drums traditionally made from rum kegs, two boula and a lead cutter, accompany a woman chantwell and chorus with shak-shak rattles. The boula card stands for the two boula drums, the membrane-drum card for the cutter and the shaker card for the shak-shak.",
  "recipe": "classic modern big drum, ritual Devotional, Afro-diasporic branch. boula, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, shaker source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
